# Whole Shoes Nduna — Website

A small, single-branch website for **Whole Shoes Nduna**, a fictional family-run
footwear store in Ilanga Mall, Nelspruit, Mpumalanga, South Africa.

## File & folder structure

```
root/
├── index.html      Home — hero, brand introduction, featured categories, sitemap
├── about.html       About Us — history, mission/vision, timeline, team
├── services.html    Services — retail & fitting, repair, delivery/collection
├── products.html     Products — photo gallery of the athletic, formal & handcrafted ranges
├── enquiry.html      Enquiry — product/service enquiry form
├── contact.html      Contact — store address, embedded map, hours, contact form
├── README.md
├── css/
│   └── style.css     Single shared stylesheet (design tokens, layout, components)
├── js/
│   └── script.js      Shared behaviour: mobile nav toggle, form validation
└── images/            (unused — all imagery is hotlinked from Unsplash; see below)
```

The brief asked for a minimum of five pages; this build includes **six**
(Home, About, Services, Products, Enquiry, Contact). The extra page, Products, is a
photo gallery of the ranges in-store and is documented here as required.

## Business changes reflected in this build

- Single branch only, at Ilanga Mall, Nelspruit, Mpumalanga (previously two branches).
- Team names throughout (About and Contact pages) are: Alfred Dunhill (Founder & Owner),
  Peter Mabuza (Store Manager), Nonhle Dube (Fitting Specialist), Siyanda Makhosi (Head
  Cobbler & Repairs) and Bradley Vuma (Sales & Customer Care). These are fictional names
  created for this student project; no photographs of real people are used to represent
  them — team "photos" are CSS-generated initials avatars instead.
- The Contact page has one location card (map + address) rather than two, per the
  request for a single-branch, basic shoe store rather than a larger multi-branch chain.
- The Services page no longer includes a corporate/bulk-order section, to keep the
  business scope appropriately small.

# Whole Shoes Nduna — Website

A small, single-branch website for **Whole Shoes Nduna**, a fictional family-run
footwear store in Ilanga Mall, Nelspruit, Mpumalanga, South Africa.

**Part 1** covered the HTML structure and content. **Part 2** (this update) covers the
CSS styling, layout, typography, colour scheme, and responsive/accessible behaviour
across desktop, tablet and mobile — see the Changelog and the "Part 2" section below.

## File & folder structure

```
root/
├── index.html      Home — hero, brand introduction, featured categories, sitemap
├── about.html       About Us — history, mission/vision, timeline, team
├── services.html    Services — retail & fitting, repair, delivery/collection
├── products.html     Products — photo gallery of the athletic, formal & handcrafted ranges
├── enquiry.html      Enquiry — product/service enquiry form
├── contact.html      Contact — store address, embedded map, hours, contact form
├── README.md
├── css/
│   └── style.css     Single shared stylesheet (design tokens, layout, components)
├── js/
│   └── script.js      Shared behaviour: mobile nav toggle, form validation
└── images/
    └── screenshots/    Desktop/tablet/mobile evidence screenshots for Part 2 (see below)
```

The brief asked for a minimum of five pages; this build includes **six**
(Home, About, Services, Products, Enquiry, Contact). The extra page, Products, is a
photo gallery of the ranges in-store and is documented here as required.

## Business changes reflected in this build

- Single branch only, at Ilanga Mall, Nelspruit, Mpumalanga (previously two branches).
- Team names throughout (About and Contact pages) are: Alfred Dunhill (Founder & Owner),
  Peter Mabuza (Store Manager), Nonhle Dube (Fitting Specialist), Siyanda Makhosi (Head
  Cobbler & Repairs) and Bradley Vuma (Sales & Customer Care). These are fictional names
  created for this student project; no photographs of real people are used to represent
  them — team "photos" are CSS-generated initials avatars instead.
- The Contact page has one location card (map + address) rather than two, per the
  request for a single-branch, basic shoe store rather than a larger multi-branch chain.
- The Services page no longer includes a corporate/bulk-order section, to keep the
  business scope appropriately small.

## Part 2: CSS styling, layout and responsive design

All styling lives in one external stylesheet, `css/style.css`, linked from every page
with `<link rel="stylesheet" href="css/style.css">` — no inline `style` attributes or
`<style>` blocks are used for layout, so the cascade only has to be reasoned about in
one place.

**Reset & base style.** The file opens with a small universal reset (`box-sizing:
border-box`, margins/padding zeroed, list styles removed, images capped at `max-width:
100%`) so every browser starts from the same baseline, followed by base rules for
`body`, headings, links and the `.container` wrapper.

**Colour scheme.** A warm, leather-and-craft palette held in CSS custom properties in
`:root` — espresso brown for the header/footer, parchment cream for the page
background, saddle-leather brown for primary actions, and an ochre gold accent used
for the stitched-line dividers that run through the site. Keeping every colour as a
variable (`var(--saddle)`, etc.) means the whole palette can be re-tuned from one
place, and it keeps contrast consistent across pages.

**Typography.** Three families with distinct jobs — Fraunces (a warm slab-serif) for
headings, Work Sans for body copy, and IBM Plex Mono for labels, prices and
navigation — set from a documented, `rem`-based type scale (`--fs-000` through
`--fs-4`, see the comment block at the top of `style.css`). Using `rem` rather than
fixed pixel sizes means the type scales correctly if a visitor increases their
browser's default font size, which matters for readability and accessibility.

**Layout.** Built with CSS Grid for page-level structure (the hero, page banners, card
grids, the project charter-style tables) and Flexbox for one-dimensional groupings
(the header bar, buttons, feature lists), using `display`, `grid-template-columns`,
`flex-direction`, `justify-content` and `align-items` throughout, per the brief.

**Visual styles & interactive states.** Buttons, links and form fields use `color`,
`background-color`, `border` and `box-shadow` for visual styling, and every
interactive element has distinct `:hover`, `:focus-visible` and `:active` states —
not just `:hover` — so the site is usable with a mouse, a keyboard, or a
switch/assistive device.

**Responsive design.** Two clear, documented breakpoints are used consistently across
the whole stylesheet instead of ad-hoc values per component:

| Breakpoint | Width | What changes |
|---|---|---|
| Desktop | default (no media query), > 900px | Multi-column grids, full horizontal nav |
| Tablet | `max-width: 900px` | Grids collapse to two columns; nav switches to the ☰ toggle menu |
| Mobile | `max-width: 600px` | Everything collapses to a single column; spacing and section padding is tightened |

Spacing (padding/margins/gaps) and font sizes are set in `rem`/`em` rather than fixed
pixels wherever they affect the overall proportions of the page, and widths use `%`
(`width:100%`, `max-width:100%`) so elements resize fluidly between breakpoints rather
than only snapping at the two media-query boundaries. Headings use `clamp()` so their
size flows smoothly with the viewport instead of jumping abruptly at a breakpoint.

**Responsive images.** Every `<img>` on the site now carries a `srcset` with four image
widths (640/960/1400/1920px) and a `sizes` attribute describing how much of the
viewport that image actually occupies at each breakpoint, so a phone downloads a
640px-wide image instead of the same 1920px file a desktop gets. This follows the
standard responsive-images pattern documented by MDN (Mozilla, 2025).

**Accessibility.** A skip-to-content link, a visible focus ring (`:focus-visible`) on
every interactive element, `aria-current="page"` on the active nav link, `aria-label`s
on icon-only controls, semantic landmark elements (`header`, `nav`, `main`, `footer`),
descriptive `alt` text on every image, and a `prefers-reduced-motion` media query that
switches off smooth-scrolling and transitions for visitors who have asked their
operating system to reduce motion (W3C WAI, 2023).

### Screenshot evidence (desktop / tablet / mobile)

`images/screenshots/` contains real browser renders of all six pages at three
viewport sizes, captured with a headless Chromium browser:

- **Desktop** — 1440 × 900
- **Tablet** — 768 × 1024 (a common portrait iPad width)
- **Mobile** — 390 × 844 (a common modern phone width)

Files are named `<page>__<breakpoint>.png`, e.g. `index__mobile.png`,
`services__tablet.png`. These were captured in a sandboxed environment without
internet access, so the Google Fonts and Unsplash photographs appear as browser
fallback fonts and broken-image placeholders in the captures — the layout, spacing,
grid collapsing and navigation behaviour they demonstrate is unaffected by that and
is exactly what renders once the page loads with a normal internet connection.
Re-capturing them on a normal connection (or simply resizing a real browser window
and using your browser's own screenshot/device-toolbar tools) will show the finished
photographs and fonts in place, if you would like fresh images for submission.

## Changelog

### Version 2 — CSS styling & responsive design (this submission)
- Added a documented `rem`-based typographic scale and spacing scale as CSS custom
  properties, replacing scattered fixed-pixel font sizes and paddings.
- Added an explicit CSS reset block at the top of `style.css`.
- Consolidated every media query in the stylesheet onto two documented breakpoints
  (900px tablet, 600px mobile) instead of the mixed 520/640/820/860px values used
  ad hoc in Part 1.
- Added `srcset`/`sizes` to every image on the site for responsive image loading.
- Added explicit `:active` states to buttons and navigation links (previously only
  `:hover`/`:focus-visible` were styled).
- Added a `prefers-reduced-motion` media query for accessibility.
- Converted card images from a fixed pixel height to `aspect-ratio`, so they scale
  proportionally rather than cropping differently at different widths.
- Captured and added desktop/tablet/mobile screenshot evidence for all six pages
  (see `images/screenshots/`).
- Updated this README with the Part 2 documentation and reference list below.

### Version 1 — HTML structure & content (Part 1)
- Initial six-page build: Home, About, Services, Products, Enquiry, Contact.
- Semantic HTML structure, navigation, forms, and Harvard-referenced imagery on every
  page (see each page's own References section).

## Images & referencing

All photographs are sourced from Unsplash and used under the Unsplash License (free to
use, attribution not legally required but provided regardless). Every page that contains
a photograph carries its own **References** section in Harvard style, listed
alphabetically by author surname, immediately above the footer. The Google Maps embed on
the Contact page is credited the same way.

Because the site is a static HTML/CSS/JS build with no backend, images are referenced by
their direct Unsplash CDN URL rather than downloaded into `images/`; that folder now
holds the Part 2 screenshot evidence instead (see above).

## References (general — techniques used to complete Part 2)

These are general technical references for the CSS/responsive-design techniques
applied across the stylesheet, as distinct from the per-page photograph references
already listed on each HTML page.

Mozilla (2025) *Responsive images*. MDN Web Docs. Available at:
https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Guides/Responsive_images
(Accessed: 16 September 2026).

Mozilla (2025) *Using CSS custom properties (variables)*. MDN Web Docs. Available at:
https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Using_CSS_custom_properties
(Accessed: 16 September 2026).

Mozilla (2025) *prefers-reduced-motion*. MDN Web Docs. Available at:
https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/@media/prefers-reduced-motion
(Accessed: 16 September 2026).

Mozilla (2025) *Using media queries*. MDN Web Docs. Available at:
https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/CSS_media_queries/Using_media_queries
(Accessed: 16 September 2026).

Unsplash (n.d.) *Unsplash License*. Available at: https://unsplash.com/license
(Accessed: 16 September 2026).

W3C Web Accessibility Initiative (2023) *Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG)
2.2*. Available at: https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/ (Accessed: 16 September 2026).

## Navigation

The navigation menu in the `<header>` is identical on all six pages and uses relative
links (`index.html`, `about.html`, `services.html`, `products.html`, `enquiry.html`,
`contact.html`), so it functions correctly regardless of which page it is opened from.
The current page is marked with `aria-current="page"` for accessibility.

## Forms

Both the Enquiry form and the general Contact form perform client-side validation only
(`js/script.js`) — there is no backend, so submissions are not actually transmitted
anywhere. Submitting a valid form shows a confirmation message; an incomplete form shows
an inline error message instead.

## Browser & device testing

Built with standard HTML5 semantic elements (`header`, `nav`, `main`, `section`,
`article`, `footer`) and CSS Grid/Flexbox with fallbacks. Layout and responsive
behaviour were verified with a headless Chromium browser at desktop, tablet and mobile
viewport widths (see Screenshot evidence, above)